<!DOCTYPE node PUBLIC "-//freedesktop//DTD D-BUS Object Introspection 1.0//EN"
 "http://www.freedesktop.org/standards/dbus/1.0/introspect.dtd">
<!-- Bus: org.templaros.Session. Path: /org/templaros/Session/Backlight. The display backlight.

     Read from sysfs (`/sys/class/backlight/<device>/{brightness,max_brightness}`) and written
     through logind's `org.freedesktop.login1.Session.SetBrightness`, never by writing the sysfs
     file directly. The sysfs node is root-owned on every distribution; logind already grants the
     active session write access to its own seat's backlight, so this needs no privilege, no
     polkit action, no udev rule shipped by TemplarOS, and nothing in `templar-hostd` — the same
     reasoning that keeps `Session.Lifecycle1` out of the privileged daemon (ADR-0003).

     A machine with no backlight — a desktop with an external monitor — is a fact, not a failure,
     and Get says so rather than raising. Implemented in
     services/templar-sessiond/src/apis/backlight.rs. -->
<node>
  <interface name="org.templaros.Backlight1">

    <!-- The backlight as JSON: { present, percent, device, raw, max_raw }.

         `present: false` is a machine with no `/sys/class/backlight` device. `device` is the
         sysfs name logind is addressed with; `raw` and `max_raw` are the hardware's own scale,
         kept because a backlight with 12 steps and one with 19200 behave differently under a
         percentage and a settings UI may want to say so. -->
    <method name="Get">
      <arg name="state_json" type="s" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Set the backlight, as a percentage, clamped to 10..=100.

         The floor is 10, not 0: a backlight really can be driven to a black screen, and on most
         panels there is then no way to see the control that would turn it back up. Turning the
         display off is a different action from dimming it, and this is not it. Ten rather than
         one because a 1% panel is unreadable in a lit room, which is the same fault a step later;
         the floor is here rather than in each caller so there is one number for all of them. -->
    <method name="SetBrightness">
      <arg name="percent" type="u" direction="in"/>
    </method>

    <!-- Move the backlight by `delta` percentage points and return where it landed. One call, so
         two clients stepping the same key cannot each undo the other. -->
    <method name="AdjustBrightness">
      <arg name="delta" type="i" direction="in"/>
      <arg name="percent" type="u" direction="out"/>
    </method>

    <!-- This service changed the backlight. Carries nothing — call Get. Changes made elsewhere
         (a firmware-handled brightness key) are not observed; clients that must not go stale
         poll Get. -->
    <signal name="Changed"/>
  </interface>
</node>
